1
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:07,348
This is eSAP — an editor for building a statistical analysis plan a machine can actually run.

2
00:00:07,803 --> 00:00:11,158
We start with a study: a phase two breast cancer trial.

3
00:00:11,611 --> 00:00:19,146
Nothing here was typed by hand. Every tab is read from the study's USDM — the CDISC Unified Study Definition Model.

4
00:00:20,942 --> 00:00:21,859
The arms.

5
00:00:23,597 --> 00:00:26,418
The objectives, and the endpoints they declare.

6
00:00:28,182 --> 00:00:29,842
The schedule of activities.

7
00:00:31,716 --> 00:00:33,051
The protocol narrative.

8
00:00:34,790 --> 00:00:39,492
This is the protocol as structured data, and the plan extends it rather than restating it.

9
00:00:41,541 --> 00:00:49,053
The primary objective is progression-free survival in post-menopausal patients with hormone-receptor positive metastatic breast cancer.

10
00:00:49,517 --> 00:00:51,978
We take the primary endpoint it declares.

11
00:00:53,654 --> 00:00:57,520
The protocol text is precise English — but it is still prose.

12
00:00:57,973 --> 00:01:04,405
To make it computable we say what kind of thing the endpoint is: a time-to-event, measured as a duration.

13
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:12,615
Now the sentence. We choose the fuller time-to-event phrase — the one naming an origin, an event and a censoring rule — and watch.

14
00:01:13,068 --> 00:01:17,538
Three definition slots appear. We don't add them; the phrase asks for them.

15
00:01:17,994 --> 00:01:20,885
The clock starts when the subject is randomised.

16
00:01:21,337 --> 00:01:27,072
The event is disease progression or death — two concepts, not one, so the slot takes both.

17
00:01:27,525 --> 00:01:29,754
And we censor at the last tumour assessment.

18
00:01:30,206 --> 00:01:35,744
Then the dimensions the analysis will cut by: the population, and the treatment groups compared.

19
00:01:39,245 --> 00:01:44,551
Notice the sentence rewriting itself as we go. It is not a caption. It is the specification.

20
00:01:46,961 --> 00:01:51,106
An endpoint says what is measured. An analysis says what is done with it.

21
00:01:51,563 --> 00:01:57,182
The library offers only methods that fit a time-to-event endpoint — here, the log-rank test.

22
00:01:57,634 --> 00:02:01,442
The specification reads in three parts. First, what goes in.

23
00:02:02,584 --> 00:02:12,127
The bindings resolve themselves: time, event, treatment, subject — each a concept, with its role in the method, and whether it is a measure or a dimension.

24
00:02:13,276 --> 00:02:19,824
Second, what is done. The model expression, written in concepts and shown against the variables that implement them.

25
00:02:20,279 --> 00:02:25,248
And the method's own configuration: the weight that makes this a log-rank rather than a Wilcoxon.

26
00:02:26,394 --> 00:02:33,372
Third, what comes out. The outputs are declared by the method itself: chi-squared, p-value, degrees of freedom.

27
00:02:34,517 --> 00:02:37,652
And from those three, the estimand assembles itself.

28
00:02:38,106 --> 00:02:43,377
Population. Treatment. The endpoint variable. A comparison of survival distributions.

29
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:47,940
The attributes ICH E9 asks for — none of them typed in.

30
00:02:50,312 --> 00:02:58,311
Everything specified so far, in one place — endpoint, method, slices, and every concept the analysis binds to.

31
00:02:59,541 --> 00:03:00,911
Nothing written twice.

32
00:03:03,598 --> 00:03:09,473
Now the plan assembles itself, section by section. And each section can reach back into the protocol.

33
00:03:09,936 --> 00:03:12,455
This is the study's own USDM narrative.

34
00:03:12,907 --> 00:03:18,840
We want the protocol's primary objective against the section on objectives, endpoints and estimands.

35
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:26,761
There it is — the text as the protocol wrote it, with the identifier it carries in the USDM.

36
00:03:28,716 --> 00:03:29,331
Link it.

37
00:03:31,217 --> 00:03:39,704
The section now carries the protocol's own words, by reference. Not copied — linked. If the protocol changes, this points at the change.

38
00:03:41,612 --> 00:03:45,641
And the analysis is not attached to the document — it is embedded in it.

39
00:03:46,093 --> 00:03:53,140
The formalised endpoint. The method. The model expression, in concepts and in variables. The outputs it will produce.

40
00:03:53,593 --> 00:03:56,124
Which means the plan is machine-readable too.

41
00:03:57,003 --> 00:04:05,397
The same document as JSON, with its provenance: the USDM, the AC/DC transformation model, the Analysis Results Standard.

42
00:04:05,885 --> 00:04:09,832
A human reads the document; a system reads this. One source.

43
00:04:12,224 --> 00:04:15,753
Progression-free survival is not collected. It is derived.

44
00:04:16,207 --> 00:04:19,771
And a derivation is rarely one step — it is a pipeline.

45
00:04:21,742 --> 00:04:24,401
Eleven steps, in the order they must run.

46
00:04:25,537 --> 00:04:33,571
First progression date, first death date, the earliest of the two. The last tumour assessment. Time to first event. The censoring indicator.

47
00:04:34,032 --> 00:04:37,840
Each names the concept it produces, not the column it writes.

48
00:04:38,991 --> 00:04:41,162
And they are not a list — they are a graph.

49
00:04:42,289 --> 00:04:48,117
The analysis at the top, its inputs beneath, and beneath those the observations they resolve from.

50
00:04:48,568 --> 00:04:53,049
Every dependency declared, so the chain can be checked before anything runs.

51
00:04:55,448 --> 00:04:59,395
A specification that cannot run is just a document. So let us run it.

52
00:04:59,852 --> 00:05:03,776
The R engine starts inside the browser — no server, no install.

53
00:05:10,984 --> 00:05:18,426
We give it the collected data: demographics, the event records, and the subject-level dataset carrying the population flags.

54
00:05:18,878 --> 00:05:23,313
One primary dataset, because the engine reads one — the others join to it.

55
00:05:23,765 --> 00:05:31,044
The population is bound to the data here, at execution: the plan names the population, the run decides which column expresses it.

56
00:05:31,497 --> 00:05:35,212
The derivation pipeline runs first, then the analysis.

57
00:05:36,658 --> 00:05:48,245
Progression-free survival, derived from collected data, compared between arms: chi-squared eighteen point six, one degree of freedom, p equals one point six times ten to the minus five.

58
00:05:48,705 --> 00:05:53,314
And the R that produced it was generated from the specification — not written by hand.
